bzip2 for OpenVMS
=================

This is bzip2 @VERSION@ built natively for OpenVMS @ARCH@, from the
unmodified release plus the patches in the vms-bzip2 project:

    https://github.com/issinoho/vms-bzip2

Kit version: @KITVERSION@.  bzip2 is free software under a BSD-style licence; see LICENSE.  The
manual is MANUAL.HTML and BZIP2.TXT; BZIP2.1 is the manual page.

What is installed
-----------------
    BZIP2$ROOT:[BIN]BZIP2.EXE, BZIP2RECOVER.EXE
    BZIP2$ROOT:[LIB]LIBBZ2.OLB      the library (libbz2)
    BZIP2$ROOT:[INCLUDE]BZLIB.H
    BZIP2$ROOT:[DOC]                this file, LICENSE, CHANGES, the manual
    BZIP2$ROOT:[000000]BZIP2$SETUP.COM   defines the commands
    SYS$STARTUP:BZIP2$STARTUP.COM      defines BZIP2$ROOT

Setting up
----------
The kit installs BZIP2$STARTUP.COM into SYS$STARTUP and runs it once.  To
define BZIP2$ROOT at every boot, the system manager adds this line to
SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM:

    $ @SYS$STARTUP:BZIP2$STARTUP.COM

Each user (or SYLOGIN.COM, for everyone) then defines the commands:

    $ @BZIP2$ROOT:[000000]BZIP2$SETUP.COM

Using it
--------
    $ bzip2 "-k" file.txt           ! file.txt.bz2, keeping file.txt
    $ bunzip2 file.txt.bz2          ! bzip2 -d
    $ bzcat file.txt.bz2            ! to SYS$OUTPUT
    $ bzip2 "-t" file.txt.bz2       ! test it

A name like file.txt.bz2 needs an ODS-5 disk.

Text and binary files
---------------------
A VMS text file (variable-length or VFC records) is compressed as text:
its records become lines ending in LF, as on Unix, so the compressed file
can be unpacked anywhere.  Decompressing gives a Stream_LF file with the
same lines.  Any other file (stream, fixed-length records: executables,
kits, binary data) is compressed byte for byte.

Using the library
-----------------
Compile with /INCLUDE=BZIP2$ROOT:[INCLUDE] and link with
BZIP2$ROOT:[LIB]LIBBZ2.OLB/LIBRARY.  The library is compiled /NAMES=(AS_IS,SHORTENED); its
header declares the API so, so a program compiled with any /NAMES links
with it.

Exit status
-----------
Under DCL a failed run exits with an error-severity status, so ON ERROR and
IF .NOT. $STATUS work; the exit code N is ($STATUS .AND. %X7F8) / 8.  Under
a GNV shell, $? is the exit code as on Unix.

Upper-case options and batch jobs
---------------------------------
Under the TRADITIONAL DCL parse style DCL upper-cases the unquoted parts of
a command and the C run-time library passes them to the program in lower
case.  Batch jobs use TRADITIONAL even when interactive logins are set to
EXTENDED.  Quote options with capitals, use the long forms, or start the
procedure with

    $ SET PROCESS/PARSE_STYLE=EXTENDED
